## Evaluation
Two separate scores are reported. The assistant-written set (15 conversations) was written after the code existed, so it is not a blind result. On 2026-11-03 it scored 15/15 conversations and 42/42 turn checks. The user-written set is in eval_user_conversations.json and is the unseen set. On 2026-11-04 the first run of the five user-written conversations scored 2/5 conversations and 2/5 turn checks (user_01 and user_03 passed). The first-run report is kept in backups\eval_results_multilingual_first_run.md and the latest report is in eval_results_multilingual.md. The three failures have two causes, found by reading the code after the run. user_02 used an intent name that does not exist (return_request; the layer uses return_exchange) and expected an answer where the layer asks for the missing order ID, which is its designed behaviour, so both of its failures are errors in the test expectation. user_04 (a question about a bill) and user_05 (a device fault) fall outside the ten intents the layer supports (order_status, cancel_order, refund, return_exchange, payment_issue, delivery_issue, product_info, human_agent, greeting, thanks), which is a scope gap. user_05 returned kind query with no intent, which is consistent with the fallback in dialogue.py that sends English text of four or more words with no recognised intent to the knowledge assistant unchanged. Neither the expectations nor the cue lists were changed to raise this score, and these five conversations are no longer unseen.

## Known limits
- The cue lists for Hindi, Malayalam, Tamil, Telugu and Kannada and all replies in those languages were written by an AI and have not been reviewed by a native speaker.
- Order ID and product code formats are assumptions (prefix lists in the config).
- Months in dates are recognised in English only.
- Names are found only after a marker phrase such as "my name is" or "mera naam".
- Correction detection uses marker words. Rule-based intent matching misses subtle phrasing.
- Four-letter words are matched with a stricter spelling rule to avoid false hits, so some four-letter typos are missed.
- Knowledge answers stay English.
- Sentiment analysis (Task 5) detects language by script as en, hi or ml, so Tamil, Telugu and Kannada messages are not analysed in their own language.
- Old session rows are not purged by this task.

## Follow-ups after the first evaluation (2026-11-04)
Masking before storage. src\multilingual\masking.py masks card numbers, phone numbers and e-mail addresses in the customer text, the stored query, the bot reply and held_text before they are written to session_data\sessions.db. Detection still runs on the unmasked text in memory. Order IDs are kept, including long numeric ones (the older mask_sensitive_data in src\pipeline\security.py does not mask +91 98765 43210 and damages ORD-1234567890, so it is not used here). The saved session state keeps order IDs, names and dates unmasked because restoring a session needs them. Limits: an unprefixed 10-digit number starting with 6 to 9 is treated as a phone number; 12-digit government ID numbers are not masked. Test: test_multilingual_masking.py.
Tickets. text_trigger (src\tickets\chat_bridge.py) reads the raw question and the answer kind, not the multilingual kind. A clarification or a social reply raises no ticket. A handoff from the multilingual layer now gets the reason customer_unresolved in any language (before this change a non-English handoff got low_confidence and its ticket lacked the previous exchange). Unresolved wording in the customer text is still detected in English only, and repeated clarifications are not counted. Test: test_multilingual_ticket_trigger.py (function level; the Streamlit screen was not checked by this test).
Findings from the user-written evaluation. user_02 was an error in the test expectation. user_04 (a question about a bill) and user_05 (a device fault) are outside the ten supported intents. When no intent is found and the text is detected as English with four or more words, the layer sends it unchanged to the knowledge assistant instead of asking for clarification (user_05 shows this).
Malayalam handoff probe. One Malayalam phrase written by the assistant (enikku oru manushyanodu samsarikkanam) was not recognised as a request for a person (kind clarify, no intent). The phrase was not reviewed by a native speaker, so this shows a possible gap, not a measured rate. Two Roman-script Hindi phrases were recognised as handoff.
Database. The six test sessions of customer cust1 (simulated dates, test sentences) were removed from sessions.db on 2026-11-04 after a backup. The test scripts and the evaluation do not write to the real database.

## Safety net, Malayalam handoff cues and holdout result (2026-11-04)
Safety net. When the layer finds no intent twice in a row (the clarification that asks what the customer needs), the second message gets a handoff with the reason repeated_unclear instead of a third question. Any other result resets the count: a recognised request, a greeting or thanks, a question about a missing order ID, or an either/or question. The ticket code treats this handoff like any other (reason customer_unresolved). Optional thresholds.unclear_before_handoff (default 2; values below 2 are ignored) is read by dialogue.py and is not set in config\multilingual_config.json. Not covered: a customer who keeps getting the "which language?" question, and one who keeps getting either/or questions. Test: test_multilingual_safety_net.py (the language is forced to English so that only the counter is tested).
Malayalam cues. user_06 (a Roman Malayalam request for a person) failed on the first run. A probe showed the message was detected as Malayalam and returned no_intent, so the cause was missing cue vocabulary, not language detection. The project author, a Malayalam speaker, then wrote six further phrasings, all built on the word manushyan. The words manushyan, manushya, manushyanod and manushyanodu were added to the Malayalam human_agent cues, together with the Roman word agent (added by the assistant, not from the author's list, as the Hindi cues already do). All seven phrasings now return a handoff, including "manushyan aano?" (are you a human?), which also hands off, as the English word human already does.
Holdout. Two further phrasings, written by the author without the assistant seeing them and with wording different from the six: user_07 passed and user_08 failed. The user-written set therefore stands at 4/8 conversations: the first five scored 2/5 on the first run, user_06 passes only because its vocabulary was added afterwards (fitted, not unseen), and the holdout scored 1/2. Two phrasings do not give a rate; they show that the Malayalam cue coverage is partial. user_08 was detected as Malayalam (confidence 0.667) and returned no_intent, which points to a missing cue word. It was left unfixed on purpose, as a finding. A probe that sent the same text twice showed the safety net working: a clarification on the first message, a handoff with repeated_unclear on the second, and ticket reason customer_unresolved at function level (the Streamlit screen was not checked for this path).
Further limits. Roman-letter human_agent cues were not added for Tamil, Telugu or Kannada (their human_agent word lists are in native script and each has one Roman phrase). Only the Malayalam human_agent additions above come from a native speaker; the other cue lists are still AI-written and unreviewed. A new Malayalam holdout written by people other than the author would give a better estimate.

## Language packs, configuration fixes and evaluation status (2026-11-07)
Name marker. entities.py now also finds a name after "I am" or "I'm", but only when the words are capitalised and are not in a small stop list, so "I am waiting" gives no name and "I am Anitha Menon" gives "Anitha Menon". Lowercase "i am anitha" is deliberately not captured (known limit). Test: test_multilingual_iam_name.py (14 checks). The unseen_06 conversation passes because of this marker; that case was written to match it, so the pass is fitted, not independent.
Configuration of languages. languages.supported_languages() falls back to English when no configured code is usable, and detect() checks default_language against the supported list. Before this fix a list such as ["xx"] raised ValueError in detect (found by test_multilingual_language_config.py, confirmed by test_multilingual_zero_languages.py, then fixed). A force_language that is not in the configured list is ignored (dialogue.py); normal detection runs instead. Test: test_multilingual_force_language.py. Switching a language off through config\multilingual_config.json was tested by removing Tamil: Tamil script is then reported unknown and the customer is asked to clarify.
Language packs. src\multilingual\packs.py loads one JSON file per language from config\languages (or the folder in MULTILINGUAL_PACKS_DIR) when dialogue.py is imported. A pack can add a new language code or replace fields of an existing one: name, reply texts, intent labels, Roman words, intent cues, marker words, person-request words, name markers, name stop words, names the customer may use for the language, and a script range. A pack is validated completely first (unknown fields, missing reply keys or labels for a new language, reply placeholders that differ from the English template, an overlapping script range, broken JSON). An invalid pack is rejected as a whole and reported by load_packs(); the other packs still load. A new language also has to be listed in "languages" in the configuration; a pack file alone does not switch it on (checked: a language in the configuration without a pack is not used). The folder config\languages does not exist yet, so no real pack is installed.
Tests: test_multilingual_packs.py (32 checks, a synthetic language "zz" with invented words) and test_multilingual_packs_autoload.py (fresh processes through bridge.handle_turn: automatic load, no pack, loading three times). These tests show that the mechanism works. They say nothing about the quality of any language's wording. Not tested: a new language written in its own script (the test pack uses Roman words only), several processes at the same time, the Streamlit page. Packs are read only when dialogue.py is imported; code that imports only languages.py does not see them. If applying a valid pack fails part way, that one language may be partly applied (it is reported as rejected).
Person requests and vocabulary (from the handoff, verified by the tests named there). Roman Malayalam requests for a person are recognised by co-occurrence cues in lexicon_person.py (test_multilingual_person_requests.py, test_multilingual_person_flow.py). Known false positive: "ente phone repair cheyyaamo?" is handed off. intents._gapped allows one extra word inside a cue phrase of three or more words (test_multilingual_phrase_gap.py); two-word phrases are excluded on purpose. Roman Malayalam words were added to the language detection list (test_multilingual_ml_vocab.py). Tamil, Telugu and Kannada have no Roman cues for asking for a person, and person-request rules exist for Malayalam only.
Concurrency. test_multilingual_concurrency.py runs 8 customers as threads in one process on one database file: no failed turn, no leaked order ID, no lost turn, no lock error. Several processes and the Streamlit page are untested.
Evaluation status. Three sets, reported separately. Assistant-written (15 conversations): 15/15, 42/42 turn checks. User-written (eval_user_conversations.json, 8 conversations): 6/8; the first run of this set scored 5/8, and the rise to 6/8 is only because the expectation of user_02 was corrected (see EXPECTATION_LOG.md); user_06 and user_08 were fitted afterwards, so they are not unseen. Unseen-set (eval_unseen_conversations.json, 18 conversations, 42 turns): 16/18, 40/42. It was written by the assistant after the code existed, so it is not blind.
Open failures, not hidden. user_04 (billing) and user_05 (device fault) are outside the ten intents (capability gaps). unseen_04 (Hindi and English mixed sentence, reported as en with mixed True, expected hi) and unseen_07 ("and that one?" with one order in context resolves to that order, expected a clarification) are open expectation questions: neither expectation was changed.
Known limits added or confirmed. Wording for Hindi, Tamil, Telugu and Kannada is AI-written and has no native review or blind test; only the Malayalam phrasings come from the user. "where is my package" is ambiguous between order_status and delivery_issue and is not resolved. Billing and device-fault intents are not added. The assistant-written sets show regressions, not generalisation.
